<h2>Languages</h2>

<p>
  Fence tags map to <a href="https://tree-sitter.github.io/">tree-sitter</a> grammars, loaded lazily and only
  for the languages a deck uses. Every language animates the same way; here is Rust.
</p>

<ul>
  <li><b>TypeScript / JavaScript:</b> <c>ts</c> <c>tsx</c> <c>js</c> <c>jsx</c></li>
  <li><b>C family:</b> <c>c</c> <c>cpp</c> (<c>cc</c> <c>cxx</c> <c>hpp</c> <c>h</c>)</li>
  <li><b>JVM:</b> <c>java</c></li>
  <li><b>Systems:</b> <c>rs</c> / <c>rust</c> · <c>go</c></li>
  <li><b>Scripting:</b> <c>py</c> · <c>lua</c> · <c>sh</c> / <c>bash</c></li>
  <li><b>Web & data:</b> <c>html</c> <c>css</c> <c>json</c></li>
  <li>anything else (<c>text</c>, <c>toml</c>, <c>yaml</c>, <c>markdown</c>, …) is a <b>static code block</b>; tabs become four spaces</li>
</ul>

<code lang=rust id=g1>
  <frame>
    fn sum(xs: &[i32]) -> i32 {
        let mut total = 0;
        for x in xs {
            total += x;
        }
        total
    }
  </frame>
  <frame>
    fn sum(xs: &[i32]) -> i32 {
        xs.iter().sum()
    }
  </frame>
</code>

<notes>
  Matching works across languages because it only sees a generic tree. New language: tags.json + main.ts.
</notes>
